n"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"step-rational-definition","justification":"This fixes the meanings of rational and irrational used in the proof.","statement":"A number is rational exactly when it is p/q for integers p,q with q nonzero; irrational means not rational."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-rational-definition"],"id":"step-assume-rational","justification":"This begins a reductio subproof by assuming the negation of irrationality.","statement":"Assume sqrt(2)=p/q for some integers p,q with q nonzero."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-assume-rational"],"id":"step-denominator-set","justification":"If q is positive, use p/q; if q is negative, use (-p)/(-q). Thus some positive integer denominator occurs.","statement":"Let S be the set of positive integers n for which sqrt(2)=m/n for some integer m; then S is nonempty."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-denominator-set"],"id":"step-minimal-denominator","justification":"By the Well-Ordering Principle, S has a least element b. By the definition of S, choose an integer a with sqrt(2)=a/b.","statement":"Choose b least in S and an integer a such that b>0 and sqrt(2)=a/b."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-minimal-denominator"],"id":"step-lowest-terms","justification":"If a and b had a common positive divisor d>1, write a=da' and b=db' with integers a',b'. Then b'=b/d is a positive integer smaller than b and sqrt(2)=a'/b', so b' is in S, contradicting the minimality of b.","statement":"a and b have no common positive divisor greater than 1."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-minimal-denominator"],"id":"step-square","justification":"Squaring sqrt(2)=a/b gives 2=a^2/b^2. Since b is positive and hence nonzero, multiplication by b^2 gives a^2=2b^2.","statement":"a^2=2b^2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-square"],"id":"step-a-even","justification":"The equation makes a^2 even. By the Division Algorithm for divisor 2, a is 2c or 2c+1. In the 